{"hash":"8a5bb1a67a9ab092","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":94.11,"said":"Look up at Nandi, Shiva’s bull, huge and brightly painted, gazing steadily toward the sanctum. Now, the story. Rama chose an auspicious hour for his worship and sent Hanuman, the mightiest of his companions, flying north to Mount Kailash, Shiva’s own mountain, to bring back a linga. Hanuman went. But Kailash is far, and he was delayed. The hour came nearer, and still there was no sign of him in the sky. Rama would not let the sacred moment pass. Then Sita knelt on the sand by the sea, and with her own hands she shaped a linga from it. Rama installed that linga of sand and worshipped it, and Shiva was pleased. That small linga, the temple holds, is the Jyotirlinga you are about to see: Ramanathaswamy, the Lord whom Rama worshipped. The Shiva Purana tells it a little differently. There, Rama shapes an earthen linga himself before the war, as he waits to cross the sea; Shiva appears in radiance, promises him victory, and at Rama’s request stays on for ever as Rameshwara. Either way, this is the Lord of Rama. But where was Hanuman? He was coming, with a linga from Kailash in his arms. Walk on into the shrine. His linga is on your right.\n\nDid you know? The Nandi here is a stucco figure about twenty-two feet long and seventeen feet high, among the largest in India.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1150},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1166,"end":1279}],"words":[[0.098,0.24,0,4],[0.35,0.168,5,7],[0.531,0.18,8,10],[0.723,0.517,11,16],[1.432,0.469,18,25],[1.913,0.361,26,30],[2.478,0.373,32,36],[2.862,0.144,37,40],[3.019,0.457,41,49],[3.487,0.553,50,57],[4.281,0.433,59,65],[4.726,0.481,66,74],[5.218,0.361,75,81],[5.591,0.096,82,85],[5.699,0.661,86,93],[7.296,0.565,95,98],[8.029,0.132,100,103],[8.173,0.589,104,109],[9.603,0.421,111,115],[10.036,0.3,116,121],[10.349,0.096,122,124],[10.457,0.649,125,135],[11.118,0.156,136,140],[11.286,0.168,141,144],[11.466,0.168,145,148],[